     "text": "```python\ndef int_to_roman(num: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Convert an integer to a Roman numeral.\n\n    The function uses a mapping of integer values to their corresponding Roman numeral symbols,\n    and iteratively subtracts the largest possible value from the input number, appending the\n    corresponding symbol to the result string.\n\n    Parameters:\n    num (int): The integer to be converted to a Roman numeral.\n\n    Returns:\n    str: The Roman numeral representation of the input integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    val_to_roman = [\n        (1000, 'M'),\n        (900, 'CM'),\n        (500, 'D'),\n        (400, 'CD'),\n        (100, 'C'),\n        (90, 'XC'),\n        (50, 'L'),\n        (40, 'XL'),\n        (10, 'X'),\n        (9, 'IX'),\n        (5, 'V'),\n        (4, 'IV'),\n        (1, 'I')\n    ]\n\n    roman_num = ''\n    for value, symbol in val_to_roman:\n        while num >= value:\n            roman_num += symbol\n            num -= value\n        if num == 0:\n            break\n    return roman_num\n```",
